<p>Smoke damage claims in Corpus Christi rarely stay inside the room where the fire started. Smoke is pushed by heat and pressure into attics, wall cavities, closets, and air ducts, and it leaves acidic soot and odor behind on nearly everything it touches. Insurers sometimes scope these losses narrowly, focusing on the burned area and offering light cleaning everywhere else. As a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster, we help property owners document where smoke actually traveled and what it takes to restore the property, so the claim is measured against the real loss.</p>

<h2>How Smoke Damage Spreads Through a Building</h2>
<p>Fire produces hot gases that rise and spread until they find cooler surfaces or a way out. Along the way, soot settles on ceilings, cabinet interiors, insulation, and fabric. A few patterns are common in Coastal Bend homes and businesses:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Attic migration.</strong> Many local homes are single-story, slab-on-grade houses with open attics. Smoke from a kitchen or garage fire can fill the attic, coating framing and insulation that no one sees during a quick walk-through.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC distribution.</strong> If the air handler was running, it may have pulled smoke into the return and pushed soot through every supply register in the building.</li>
<li><strong>Shared walls.</strong> In duplexes, apartments, condos, and strip centers, smoke from one unit often reaches the next through plenums, attic spaces, and wall penetrations.</li>
<li><strong>Neighboring fires.</strong> A fire next door or a large grass fire can drive smoke in through soffit vents, gaps around windows, and open doors.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our coastal humidity can make residue harder to remove and odor more persistent, especially on porous surfaces. That is one reason a cleaning-only scope does not always restore a property to its pre-loss condition.</p>

<h2>Types of Smoke and Soot Residue</h2>
<p>Different fuels leave different residues, and the residue type affects how surfaces must be cleaned. The table below is a general guide that restoration professionals commonly use.</p>
<table>
