<tbody>
<tr><td>Dry, powdery soot</td><td>Fast, hot fires burning paper or wood</td><td>Easier to vacuum, but fine particles reach far into ducts and contents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wet, smeary soot</td><td>Slow, smoldering fires burning plastics or rubber</td><td>Smears when wiped; often needs specialized cleaning and may stain permanently</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protein residue</td><td>Kitchen fires involving food</td><td>Nearly invisible but carries strong odor and can discolor paint and cabinets</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fuel or petroleum residue</td><td>Fires involving fuels, oils, or equipment</td><td>Dense and sticky; common in garage, shop, and commercial losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wildfire or exterior smoke</td><td>Grass fires or fires on neighboring property</td><td>Enters through vents and openings; often affects attics and HVAC systems</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What a Complete Smoke Damage Scope Often Includes</h2>
<p>A well-documented smoke claim typically goes beyond wiping walls and repainting. Depending on the loss, the scope may need to address:</p>
<ul>
