<li>HVAC inspection, duct cleaning, and replacement of components that cannot be cleaned</li>
<li>Sealing and repainting ceilings, walls, trim, and cabinet interiors</li>
<li>Cleaning or replacing flooring, especially carpet and pad that hold odor</li>
<li>Deodorization, which may include thermal fogging, ozone, or hydroxyl treatment by a qualified contractor</li>
<li>Contents cleaning, pack-out, and storage, plus replacement of items that cannot be restored</li>
<li>Electronics evaluation where soot may have entered the housing</li>
<li>Additional living expense or business income while the property is unusable</li>
</ul>
<p>Firefighting often leaves water damage too. If water from hoses or sprinklers soaked drywall and flooring, that part of the loss belongs in the claim as well. See our <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> pages for how those pieces fit together.</p>

