<h2>Common Disputes in Smoke Damage Claims</h2>
<h3>Clean versus replace</h3>
<p>This is the most frequent disagreement. An insurer may propose cleaning drapes, upholstered furniture, or a mattress, while the owner reports that the odor remains after cleaning. Documenting the result of cleaning, not just the plan to clean, is often what resolves it.</p>
<h3>Limiting the affected area</h3>
<p>Some estimates include only the room of origin and the adjoining rooms. Photos of soot inside closed cabinets, on attic framing, and at distant supply registers can show that smoke traveled farther. Wipe samples or reports from an indoor environmental professional can add weight when the residue is not obvious.</p>
<h3>Odor that returns</h3>
<p>Odor can reappear on hot, humid days, which are common in Corpus Christi most of the year. If odor comes back after deodorization, that should be reported to the insurer in writing promptly.</p>
<h3>Matching and uniform appearance</h3>
<p>When some cabinets, flooring, or wall finishes are replaced and others are cleaned, the result may not match. How matching is handled depends on the policy language and the facts; it is worth raising early rather than after repairs are complete.</p>

<h2>Documentation That Supports a Smoke Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li>Photograph every room, including inside cabinets, closets, the attic, and HVAC registers, before cleaning begins.</li>
<li>Keep a written inventory of affected contents, with approximate age and cost where you can.</li>
<li>Save receipts for hotel stays, meals, and other additional expenses.</li>
<li>Request copies of any fire department report and keep all insurer correspondence.</li>
<li>Ask contractors and cleaners for written reports describing what they found and what they recommend.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through these items in more detail. For an overview of how depreciation affects contents and building items, read <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>How Corpus Christi Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
